{ Tests for snappy, lz4 and xxHash32 (Kafka.Compression); gzip's are in
  Kafka.RecordBatchTests, and the batches the Java producer compressed with
  each codec in Kafka.ProduceFetchTests.

  - xxHash32: the published vectors and a seed, the values of the xxhash
    package for Python.
  - snappy: raw blocks written by the reference library (cramjam's snappy,
    for Python) read back; every kind of element built by hand (literal
    lengths of 1 to 4 extra bytes, copies with 1-, 2- and 4-byte offsets,
    overlapping copies); round trips of several sizes and shapes; the bad
    blocks refused (cut, a copy from before the start, more or less than the
    length declared, a length no block that size can hold); the limit.
    xerial's framing as Java's SnappyOutputStream writes it (the header,
    32 KiB chunks), read back with concatenated streams and a raw block
    (librdkafka's), and its bad framings refused.
  - lz4: frames written by the reference library (python-lz4) with every
    option Java's reader takes (checksums, content size, 4 MiB blocks, HC
    compression, several blocks, a block stored uncompressed); the frame as
    Java writes it (FLG 60, BD 40, its header checksum); KIP-57's broken
    header checksum and what Java refuses (dependent blocks, a dictionary id,
    reserved bits, another version), refused; corrupt frames; single blocks
    by hand and from the reference; round trips; the limit.
  - Corrupt input (bytes changed, cut, inserted) either decodes or raises
    EKafkaCompression, never anything else. The same, 16 times as many and
    with the unit built with range and overflow checks on, ran while this
    was written: no index outside the data.
  - A record batch compressed with each codec decodes to its records.

  The vectors are hex constants made once with the reference libraries
  (lz4 4.4.5, cramjam 2.14.0 and xxhash for Python); Text and Pseudorandom
  make the same inputs here as the script that wrote them. This library's
  own compressed output was also checked against those libraries while it
  was written (every shape below, both directions); the tests here read
  their bytes and round-trip this library's.

  DUnitX master: the FPCUnit mirror in tests/Unit/fpc is generated by
  tools/gen_fpc_mirror.py. }

